WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.370 --> 00:00:41.570
In his obituary, the New York Times called
Robert Franklin Stroud “probably America’s

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:46.899
most famous convict.” He rose to notoriety
late in life, after author and former corrections

00:00:46.899 --> 00:00:52.950
officer Thomas E. Gaddis wrote a book about
him in 1955 titled “Birdman of Alcatraz.”

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:57.370
A few years later, this turned into a movie
of the same name starring Burt Lancaster in

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:02.220
the title role. This was a major Hollywood
production, which proved popular with moviegoers

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:06.020
and critics and received four Academy Award
nominations.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.460
Both the movie and the book were instrumental
in shaping the persona of the Birdman of Alcatraz

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:15.680
that the American public came to know and
even admire. In both of them, Robert Stroud

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.650
was portrayed in a positive, even heroic light.
Here was a self-taught man of superior intellect

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:27.090
with a lot to offer who did something bad
once, but was ready and willing to make it

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.440
up to society.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:34.000
This view, although it turned Robert Stroud
into a media sensation, did not line up with

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.480
reality. At least, not according to the guards
and inmates who actually knew him. One said

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.810
that he “was not a sweetheart” like the
Birdman in the movie and that “Burt Lancaster

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.740
owes us all an apology.” Others said that
the real Stroud was a “vicious killer”,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:52.560
or “a guy that liked chaos and turmoil.”

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.680
The doctor who did his official psychiatric
evaluation in 1943 concluded that Stroud was

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.130
a psychopath with an IQ of 112. Clearly, there
is a lot more to the tale of the Birdman of

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:11.329
Alcatraz than what has been publicized and
today we will try to get the real story.

00:02:11.329 --> 00:02:16.230
Early Years

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.870
Robert Franklin Stroud was born on January
28, 1890, in Seattle, Washington, to Benjamin

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.709
Franklin Stroud and Elizabeth Jane McCartney.

00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:29.239
Not much is known about his childhood. He
had a younger brother and two older step-sisters

00:02:29.239 --> 00:02:33.930
from his mother’s previous marriage. Life
at home was not a happy one because Ben Stroud

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:38.310
was an alcoholic and an abuser and his children
were often the target of his rage.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:43.430
When he was 13, Robert Stroud had had enough
of his father’s persecution so he ran away

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:48.079
from home. He drifted from one place to another
for a few years, before eventually making

00:02:48.079 --> 00:02:52.870
his way to the Alaska Territory when he was
18. At first, he found an honest job working

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.400
on a railroad gang. This kept him on the move
and he ended up in the town of Cordova. There,

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.879
he met an older woman named Kitty O’Brien
who worked as a dance hall entertainer and

00:03:02.879 --> 00:03:07.730
occasional prostitute. Stroud began serving
as her pimp, although the exact nature of

00:03:07.730 --> 00:03:12.049
their relationship is still up for debate.
Some say that it was strictly professional,

00:03:12.049 --> 00:03:16.469
while others believe that the two were romantically
involved. Either way, they spent a lot of

00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:21.641
time together and, in 1908, they decided to
relocate to Juneau which, back then, was a

00:03:21.641 --> 00:03:23.049
booming gold town.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:28.120
It was here that Robert Stroud first started
to show his violent nature. He became angry

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:33.749
with an acquaintance of his, a bartender identified
as Charlie Van Dahmer or Damer. The exact

00:03:33.749 --> 00:03:39.169
problem is uncertain. Some sources say that
Van Dahmer beat up Kitty O’Brien, others

00:03:39.169 --> 00:03:44.290
that he refused to pay her after using her
services, or maybe even both. Regardless,

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:47.760
this was not something Stroud was willing
to let slide.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:52.249
He went to Van Dahmer’s shack to confront
him. He was armed with a .38-caliber pistol.

00:03:52.249 --> 00:03:57.319
He waited for the bartender to arrive home
and killed him with a shot to the heart. Afterwards,

00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:00.139
Stroud turned himself in at the city marshal.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:04.549
His mother heard of what had happened and
hired a lawyer to defend her son. They were

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:09.889
able to strike a deal where Stroud would avoid
a charge of first-degree murder with the possibility

00:04:09.889 --> 00:04:14.980
of the death penalty by pleading guilty to
manslaughter. Both mother and son were hoping

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:19.539
for a light sentence of only a few years in
prison, but this was not to be. The federal

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:24.789
judge who presided over his case was named
E. E. Cushman. He was new on the job and eager

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:29.930
to show everyone that he was hard on crime,
particularly to those who used violence in

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.230
his jurisdiction. He saw Stroud as the perfect
man to make an example out of so, on August

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:41.979
23, 1909, Cushman sentenced him to the maximum
of 12 years, to be served at the McNeil Island

00:04:41.979 --> 00:04:47.629
Federal Penitentiary on the Puget Sound. Stroud
didn’t know it yet, but he would never see

00:04:47.629 --> 00:04:51.080
life outside a prison again.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:56.650
Life Behind Bars

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:01.050
Robert Stroud did not serve a lot of his sentence
at McNeil Island. After his arrival, it did

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:05.370
not take long before he developed a reputation
with the guards and the other inmates of being

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:10.400
an angry, dangerous man. On one occasion,
he assaulted a hospital orderly whom he believed

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.139
had reported Stroud to the prison administration
for trying to obtain narcotics by using threats

00:05:16.139 --> 00:05:20.500
and intimidation. On another occasion, Stroud
got into a fight and stabbed a prisoner who,

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.729
allegedly, ratted him out for stealing food.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:26.639
This attack earned Stroud an additional six
months added to his sentence, but he would

00:05:26.639 --> 00:05:32.110
not serve them at McNeil. Instead, in 1912,
Stroud and dozens of other inmates were transferred

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:37.690
to a newer prison - the maximum-security Leavenworth
Federal Penitentiary in Kansas. Leavenworth

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:42.470
would go on to become one of the most infamous
prisons in the country but, at the time, it

00:05:42.470 --> 00:05:46.159
had only been opened for less than a decade
and some of the cell blocks were still under

00:05:46.159 --> 00:05:50.229
construction. However, this move was necessary
due to prison overcrowding.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:55.289
Although Stroud had left school at age 12,
he soon discovered that his time behind bars

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:59.599
represented a good opportunity to continue
his learning. He took various long-distance

00:05:59.599 --> 00:06:04.349
courses from the Kansas State Agricultural
College and excelled at most of them, despite

00:06:04.349 --> 00:06:09.280
his third grade education. These included
hard sciences like mathematics, structural

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.000
engineering, and astronomy, but also spiritual
courses like theosophy.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.030
The progress Stroud was making was derailed
in 1915 when he was diagnosed with Bright’s

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:23.069
disease, an illness that causes chronic inflammation
of the kidneys. Stroud had struggled with

00:06:23.069 --> 00:06:27.659
the pain and the other symptoms for years,
but it finally reached a point where he became

00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:32.610
bedridden and, for a while, even feared that
he might die. Eventually, he started to slowly

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:37.699
recover, but he was left weakened and gaunt,
not to mention that the pain was still there

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:42.250
and was steadily turning Stroud into a more
bitter and angry man.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.910
Any hope he still had of ever walking as a
free man went away the following year after

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:50.610
he killed again. On March 26, 1916, Stroud
attacked one of the prison guards in the mess

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:55.060
hall and murdered him by plunging a butcher
knife into his chest. That guard’s name

00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:00.330
was Andrew Turner and the exact circumstances
behind his death are still somewhat unclear.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:05.530
Some sources which are more favorable to Stroud
painted Turner as an arrogant bully who enjoyed

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:10.479
taunting and exerting his authority over the
inmates. Allegedly, Stroud became infuriated

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:15.409
after being denied seeing his brother who
came to visit him for the first time. He started

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:20.560
lashing out verbally and Turner reported him
which caused the inmate to lose all his visiting

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.560
privileges. In turn, this led to the aforementioned
altercation which resulted in the guard’s

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:26.560
death.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.699
Another version of the story also mentions
Stroud as being enraged at not being able

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:35.319
to see his brother. Turner, however, only
did something trivial by telling the prisoner

00:07:35.319 --> 00:07:40.090
to stop talking in the mess hall, but this
was enough to make him snap and kill the guard

00:07:40.090 --> 00:07:42.879
in front of over a thousand witnesses.

00:07:42.879 --> 00:07:47.960
Escaping the Death Penalty

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:52.730
Obviously, Stroud was found guilty of first-degree
murder. In his first trial, he was sentenced

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:57.449
to death by hanging. Fortunately for him,
his mother had, once again, hired a good lawyer

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:02.960
to defend him. They immediately filed an appeal
and managed to get the trial invalidated on

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.199
a technicality. Stroud was safe, but only
temporarily, as another trial was scheduled

00:08:07.199 --> 00:08:08.199
for the following year.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:13.061
Meanwhile, Elizabeth Stroud was doing everything
she could on her end to save her son. She

00:08:13.061 --> 00:08:17.180
wrote to everyone who would listen, trying
to get them on her side and against the death

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:18.180
penalty.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.599
When the time came for the second trial, Stroud’s
lawyer used the insanity plea as his defense,

00:08:22.599 --> 00:08:27.590
arguing that his client was mentally incompetent.
He also claimed that Stroud acted in self-defense

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:30.939
as Turner began attacking him with his club
first.

00:08:30.939 --> 00:08:36.870
On May 22, 1917, Robert Stroud was, once again,
found guilty of Andrew Turner’s murder.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:41.370
This time, however, he was sentenced to life
in prison and not the death penalty. Stroud

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.390
was still not happy with the result, though,
as he believed that his defense had been unable

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:49.529
to present evidence which was critical to
his case. He instructed his lawyer to file

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:55.360
an appeal again, and the trial was, once more,
invalidated and a new one scheduled for the

00:08:55.360 --> 00:08:56.360
following year.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:00.470
The third trial did not go as Stroud was hoping.
We’re not sure if it was because too much

00:09:00.470 --> 00:09:05.800
time had passed, or maybe annoyance that his
client filed for another appeal, but Stroud’s

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.899
lawyer lost interest in the case. When the
hearing began, he failed to show up, prompting

00:09:09.899 --> 00:09:15.089
the judge to disqualify him and assign new
lawyers to the defense. More than that, Stroud

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:19.540
discovered that his lawyer had already negotiated
with the prosecution without his knowledge,

00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:23.880
agreeing to enter a guilty plea for second-degree
murder. This was probably the best he could

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.320
have hoped for, but Stroud still protested
the plea because it was done without his approval.

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:33.279
Given the new circumstances, the judge decided
to delay the trial for a few months.

00:09:33.279 --> 00:09:38.110
In June 1918, the court proceedings resumed.
Stroud was able to present all the new evidence

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:43.410
and witnesses he wanted but, surprise, surprise,
he was still found guilty of first-degree

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:48.279
murder. Not only that, but this time he was
sentenced to death again. Another appeal was

00:09:48.279 --> 00:09:52.440
filed by Stroud’s new lawyers and the Supreme
Court ordered a temporary stay of execution

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:57.750
while the case was analyzed. However, in November
1919, the Supreme Court not only upheld the

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.649
decision, but also disallowed any further
hearings on the matter. Robert Stroud’s

00:10:01.649 --> 00:10:07.680
execution was scheduled for April 23, 1920.
At this point, those previous offers were

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.610
probably looking pretty good.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:13.630
After the third trial had concluded, there
was just one person who still had not given

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:18.820
up on Stroud - his mother. In order to save
her son, Elizabeth had to find a way to appeal

00:10:18.820 --> 00:10:24.770
to a power greater than the Supreme Court.
There was only one solution - Executive Clemency.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.680
She wanted to go straight to the President
of the United States and ask to commute Robert’s

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:30.680
death sentence.

00:10:30.680 --> 00:10:35.440
At the time, the President was Woodrow Wilson,
but he was bedridden following a stroke and

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.339
his wife, Edith, handled many of his duties.
She met with Elizabeth Stroud in person and,

00:10:40.339 --> 00:10:45.170
eventually, stopped the execution eight days
before it was scheduled to take place.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:49.170
Becoming the Birdman

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:54.440
The trials and appeals were over. Robert Stroud
managed to escape the death penalty, but he

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.139
was sentenced to spend the rest of his life
in solitary confinement. He was placed in

00:10:58.139 --> 00:11:02.580
the so-called “Isolation Building” at
Leavenworth where he had restricted contact

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:06.420
with people and could not move around the
prison grounds freely. That being the case,

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.310
the warden did allow Stroud to continue his
education and scientific interests.

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:14.870
The prisoner’s life took a significant turn
for the better one day in June, 1920, while

00:11:14.870 --> 00:11:19.070
Stroud was out walking through the exercise
yard. He had found what remained of a nest

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:23.269
which got blown away during a storm. The nest
was destroyed, but it still contained three

00:11:23.269 --> 00:11:28.770
live baby sparrows. Stroud decided to try
and nurse them back to health and, thus, the

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.990
Birdman of Alcatraz was born. As you are about
to see, the “Birdman of Leavenworth” would

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:36.780
have been a far more appropriate name for
him, but I guess it didn’t have the same

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:37.780
ring to it.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:42.150
Anyway, Stroud brought the three sparrow chicks
to his cell, improvised a nest and started

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:46.600
feeding his new roommates. Some have described
this event as Stroud finding a new lease on

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:51.100
life as ornithology became his new passion.
He read all the books on the subject that

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:54.940
he could get his hands on. He learned how
to properly care for birds and was allowed

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:56.210
to keep canaries.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:12:00.890
Fortunately for Stroud, the warden was initially
pleased and even encouraged his new interest.

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.350
It wasn’t long before the inmate started
breeding his own canaries and even selling

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:07.860
them to people who visited the prison. Over
the following years, the Birdman’s aviary

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.870
had become one of the main attractions at
Leavenworth. At its height, Stroud packed

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:16.820
in over three hundred canaries in his cell,
keeping them in cages he made out of cigar

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:21.899
boxes. His business lasted until 1931 when
a new ruling said that federal prisoners were

00:12:21.899 --> 00:12:26.180
no longer able to conduct for-profit enterprises
while incarcerated.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:30.160
His day job may have gone away, but this did
not dull Stroud’s enthusiasm for birds.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:35.399
He simply focused more on the theoretical
side. Being in prison, there were obviously

00:12:35.399 --> 00:12:40.670
still a lot of things that Stroud could not
obtain, so he improvised. He studied pharmacology

00:12:40.670 --> 00:12:45.329
and medicine so he could make his own remedies
and antiseptics. When a bird died, he dissected

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:49.510
it using his fingernails. He began corresponding
with thousands of other breeders and bird

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:53.759
experts and even started writing articles
that were good enough to be published in journals.

00:12:53.759 --> 00:12:58.040
One of the people Stroud corresponded with
was a canary breeder from Indiana named Della

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:03.040
Mae Jones who had been very impressed with
the inmate’s avian know-how. Soon enough,

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:07.639
she started visiting Leavenworth to see Stroud
and the two even launched a business together

00:13:07.639 --> 00:13:12.020
selling his bird medicine. It did well, but
it lasted only a short while before he was

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.079
forced to give it up in 1931.

00:13:14.079 --> 00:13:18.130
By this point, it was fair to say that the
prison warden and the entire Federal Bureau

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:22.830
of Prisons, for that matter, were growing
tired of Stroud and his birds and would have

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:26.829
liked to use this opportunity to get rid of
them altogether. However, the Birdman did

00:13:26.829 --> 00:13:31.079
not take this decision lying down. Him and
Jones (mostly Jones, if we’re being honest)

00:13:31.079 --> 00:13:35.500
began a letter writing campaign to raise awareness
of his problem which resonated with many other

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:40.279
breeders and bird lovers across the country.
Jones obtained 50,000 signatures for a petition

00:13:40.279 --> 00:13:43.970
which she sent to the President of the United
States. We’re not sure if the president

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:48.130
was ever actually made aware of the issue,
but all the added publicity was enough to

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:52.389
get the Federal Bureau of Prisons to back
off. They didn’t allow Stroud to make profits

00:13:52.389 --> 00:13:57.810
off his business again, but he could keep
the birds, the scientific research, and equipment

00:13:57.810 --> 00:14:00.000
and was even moved to a bigger cell.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:04.459
With the business side of his career gone,
Stroud had nothing else to do but concentrate

00:14:04.459 --> 00:14:08.389
on the research. He compiled everything he
learned about the death and disease of the

00:14:08.389 --> 00:14:13.940
birds he studied into a 60,000-word manuscript
which he smuggled out of prison. In the outside

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:19.191
world, Jones helped get it published in 1933
under the title Diseases of Canaries. Almost

00:14:19.191 --> 00:14:24.209
a decade later, he wrote and published Stroud’s
Digest on the Diseases of Birds which was,

00:14:24.209 --> 00:14:27.569
more or less, the same book with some new
and updated information.

00:14:27.569 --> 00:14:32.779
The original title from 1933 was moderately
successful and contained plenty of useful

00:14:32.779 --> 00:14:37.019
information, most of which is still relevant
today. However, Stroud claimed that he had

00:14:37.019 --> 00:14:41.759
been cheated by his publisher and that he
had not received any royalties from sales.

00:14:41.759 --> 00:14:46.519
He used the only weapon he had at his disposal
- public opinion. He publicized the issue

00:14:46.519 --> 00:14:51.029
which prompted his publisher to complain to
prison authorities who, again, began looking

00:14:51.029 --> 00:14:55.170
for a way of making the Birdman problem go
away forever.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:59.040
Allegedly, this was the first time that they
tried to have him sent to Alcatraz, but Stroud

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:03.350
managed to prevent it through a legal loophole.
He learned that prisoners had the right to

00:15:03.350 --> 00:15:08.510
remain in Kansas if they were married to someone
still living in the state so, later that same

00:15:08.510 --> 00:15:11.570
year, he and Della Mae Jones tied the knot.

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:16.060
Apparently, this enraged his mother. We cannot
tell you why, exactly, maybe she believed

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:21.569
that women were the source of all her son’s
troubles, or maybe she held an unhealthy resentment

00:15:21.569 --> 00:15:25.130
for any other woman who was part of Robert’s
life. Although she had been his strongest

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:30.010
supporter for decades, Elizabeth Stroud completely
turned on her son and even testified against

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:35.319
him at his first parole hearing in 1937. Unsurprisingly,
his parole was denied.

00:15:35.319 --> 00:15:39.839
The problems came one after another in the
years that followed. First, Stroud fell ill

00:15:39.839 --> 00:15:43.200
and, again, it was serious enough that it
was thought he might die. He recovered, but

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:48.240
a few years later his world changed forever.
The prison board finally had the excuses they

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:53.600
needed to get rid of him. On December 15,
1942, Stroud was woken up in the early morning

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:56.860
and told he was being transferred to Alcatraz.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:02.110
Welcome to the Rock

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:07.220
Allegedly, Robert Stroud only had ten minutes
warning that he was being moved from Leavenworth.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:11.320
He had to leave behind all of his belongings
which were sent to his brother. As for the

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:15.959
exact reason, it wasn’t really explained
in detail, citing merely a “serious breach

00:16:15.959 --> 00:16:20.100
of prison rules.” Supposedly, Stroud stood
accused of using the special equipment he

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:24.220
was afforded to make moonshine or shivs for
the other inmates. Whether or not he actually

00:16:24.220 --> 00:16:28.639
did this, we’re not sure, but true or not,
it allowed the Leavenworth officials to unload

00:16:28.639 --> 00:16:30.480
one of their problem prisoners.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:34.350
Alcatraz had a much stricter set of rules.
While Stroud was afforded some privileges

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:39.029
like correspondence, access to the library,
and even visitations, he was no longer allowed

00:16:39.029 --> 00:16:43.600
to keep birds. That’s right. Even though
he is known as the “Birdman of Alcatraz,”

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:48.959
Robert Stroud never actually had any birds
during the 17 years he spent imprisoned there.

00:16:48.959 --> 00:16:53.399
Instead, he kept on studying and writing.
Supposedly, he learned multiple foreign languages

00:16:53.399 --> 00:16:57.269
during his time there, and he also completed
two new manuscripts. One of them was simply

00:16:57.269 --> 00:17:01.770
titled Bobbie and it was an autobiography
of his life before being incarcerated. The

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:06.750
second one was called Looking Outward: A History
of the U.S. Prison System from Colonial Times

00:17:06.750 --> 00:17:11.449
to the Formation of the Bureau of Prisons.
This one proved to be much more problematic.

00:17:11.449 --> 00:17:15.070
Stroud wanted to publish it, but his request
was denied by the Director of the Federal

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:20.439
Bureau of Prisons. The book was deemed libelous
as it named multiple wardens and guards and

00:17:20.439 --> 00:17:25.140
the illegal actions they were allegedly involved
in such as taking bribes or beating up prisoners.

00:17:25.140 --> 00:17:29.620
It was also considered obscene as it contained
graphic sexual content.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:34.429
Stroud filed a lawsuit but actually died before
a decision was reached. Decades later, his

00:17:34.429 --> 00:17:37.821
lawyer took up possession of the manuscripts,
but still couldn’t find a publisher. It

00:17:37.821 --> 00:17:42.039
wasn’t until just a few years ago that some
parts of the book were published for the first

00:17:42.039 --> 00:17:43.039
time.

00:17:43.039 --> 00:17:47.409
Back in Alcatraz, Stroud spent the first six
years in segregation in D Block. For the next

00:17:47.409 --> 00:17:51.480
11, he was confined to the hospital wing of
the prison. He kept writing numerous petitions

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:55.240
- to the president, to the Supreme Court,
to the attorney general, to anyone who could

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.320
help him - in order to get a release, but
all his efforts failed. He tried to commit

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:03.880
suicide once by overdosing on pain medication,
but he didn’t take enough.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:08.309
Robert Stroud was never able to get out of
prison, but in his final years he got the

00:18:08.309 --> 00:18:13.310
next best thing. Due to his failing health,
in 1959 he was transferred to the minimum-security

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:18.290
Medical Center for Federal Prisoners in Springfield,
Missouri. For a man who had spent most of

00:18:18.290 --> 00:18:23.140
his life in solitary confinement, this was
an almost unreal experience. He could walk

00:18:23.140 --> 00:18:27.180
the grounds at his leisure. He had a park
where he could spend his time. He saw his

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:32.100
first TV program. Stroud later concluded that
he saw more people and spoke more words in

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:37.030
those last few years in Springfield than he
did in the previous four decades of his life.

00:18:37.030 --> 00:18:41.610
By this point, Thomas Gaddis had already published
his book about him and work had begun on the

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.970
movie. The Birdman of Alcatraz was one of
the most famous criminals in the country.

00:18:45.970 --> 00:18:51.820
And yet, when Robert Stroud died on November
21, 1963, there were few mentions of him as

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:56.809
he had the unfortunate timing of dying one
day before the assassination of President

00:18:56.809 --> 00:19:10.529
John F. Kennedy.

